---
name: rema
description: Execute commands, run training, and check GPU status on remote machines through a shared mount using ReMA. Requires a running worker on the target machine.
---

# ReMA — Remote Machine Access

Execute commands on remote machines through a shared mount. All machines must mount the same data volume.

Source: https://github.com/Disapole-Xiao/ReMA
Local checkout: `/path/to/ReMA` (replace with your repository location).

## Prerequisites

- `REMA_DIR` environment variable must be set (via `~/.rema_config`, which is created by `install.sh`)
- To activate: `source ~/.rema_config` or `source ~/.bashrc` (if added during install)
- Remote machine must have a worker running (`rema start <name>`)

## Quick Reference

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `rema start <name>` | Start worker in current directory |
| `rema stop <name>` | Stop accepting jobs, wait for running jobs, then exit |
| `rema stop <name> -f` | Terminate this worker's jobs, then exit |
| `rema restart <name> [-f]` | Restart the remote worker in its original directory; drain jobs, or terminate them with `-f` |
| `rema status <name>` | idle / busy / off |
| `rema run <name> [--async] -- <cmd>` | Execute command (sync: real-time; async: immediate return) |
| `rema jobs <name>` | List only running jobs |
| `rema jobs <name> -a` | List all jobs, including queued tasks and history (`--all` also works) |
| `rema kill <name> <job_id>` | Terminate a running job; job ID is mandatory |
| `rema log <name>` | Show latest job output |
| `rema log <name> <job_id>` | Show specific job output |
| `rema list` | Show all machines and status |
| `rema rm <name>` | Remove a machine (must be off) |

## Installation

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Disapole-Xiao/ReMA.git
cd ReMA
git fetch origin --tags
git checkout v1.0.0
bash install.sh
# When prompted, set REMA_DIR to a directory on your shared mount.
source ~/.bashrc
```

To uninstall: `bash uninstall.sh`

## Usage

Before running commands, the remote machine must have a worker running:
```bash
# On remote machine, in the desired working directory:
cd /your/project
rema start gpu-server
```

Then from any machine:
```bash
# Check status
rema status gpu-server

# Run command (sync, real-time output, same exit code as command)
rema run gpu-server -- nvidia-smi
rema run gpu-server -- "cd project && python train.py"

# Run command (async, returns immediately, retrieve with log)
rema run gpu-server --async -- "sleep 10 && python train.py"

# Check output of async job (latest or by ID)
rema log gpu-server
rema log gpu-server 1775844820654_98397_17396_20435_abCDef1234

# List all machines
rema list

# On gpu-server, stop the worker and wait for its jobs to finish
rema stop gpu-server
# From any client, remove the off worker's shared records
rema rm gpu-server
```

## Configuration

Set via `~/.rema_config` or environment variable. Priority: env var > `~/.rema_config` > default.

| Variable | Default | Description |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| `REMA_DIR` | required | Shared directory path (auto-set by `install.sh`) |
| `REMA_LOG_KEEP_DAYS` | `7` | Auto-delete old logs (days) |
| `REMA_LOCAL_DIR` | `/tmp/rema` | Local worker state directory |
| `REMA_POLL_INTERVAL` | `5` | Worker loop sleep in seconds; heartbeat updates in the same loop |
| `REMA_OUTPUT_POLL_INTERVAL` | `1` | Client output/control polling interval in seconds |

To change log retention: `echo 'export REMA_LOG_KEEP_DAYS=30' >> ~/.rema_config && source ~/.rema_config`

## Key Behaviors

- Commands execute in the directory where `rema start` was run
- Sync mode returns the same exit code as the command
- `rema list` shows all registered machines; `off` means worker is not running or heartbeat expired
- Each job gets a unique ID; use `rema log <name> <job_id>` for specific jobs
- To remove a machine, it must be `off` — run `rema stop` first if idle/busy
- Multiple workers can run on the same machine under different names
- A busy worker accepts new jobs and runs them concurrently, each in its own Bash process group
- Sync/async only changes client waiting; both modes keep separate logs and exit codes
- `kill` requires a specific running job ID; machine-only `kill` is not supported
- Ordinary `stop` drains running jobs; `stop -f` terminates all jobs owned by that worker
- Each start accepts only newly submitted jobs; earlier requests are never replayed
- `jobs` shows only running jobs; `jobs <name> -a` includes history; `unknown` means an earlier worker's execution outcome could not be confirmed
- The latest log is chosen by submission ID, not last output modification time
- Normal submissions require protocol 2; restart workers when upgrading
- Develop in the branch and directory requested by the user. Avoid changing code used by active workers; use isolated state directories for tests.

## Existing installations and remote restart

- An installed CLI symlink follows changes to the same repository path. Running workers load new code only after restart. Check `rema list` first, then `rema restart <name>` from any host sharing storage. This restarts ReMA, not the operating system; offline workers still require host access to start.
- Default restart closes new submissions, drains running tasks, and starts in the original working directory. `restart <name> -f` terminates only that worker's tracked jobs; it can escalate an existing graceful restart. Earlier jobs are never replayed. Interrupting the client after handoff does not cancel restart.
- The command stages the calling CLI's code on shared storage and runs a detached helper on the worker host. It supports the initial remote upgrade from early protocol-2 workers and legacy protocol-1 workers. Legacy upgrade is graceful only (`-f` is rejected); finish submitting through old clients first because they cannot honor the new restart lock.
- Restart upgrades the worker, without rewriting the target host's installed CLI path. It preserves other inherited environment variables and saved explicit ReMA overrides, while reloading current config/defaults. Workers predating saved overrides reload ReMA settings from config/defaults; persist any old shell-only customization in the target config before the first upgrade.
- Check the target's `protocol`, `version`, and `poll_interval` files in `$REMA_DIR/<name>/`; client `--version` alone does not identify worker code. The default worker interval is 5 seconds, plus processing time; client output polling remains 1 second.
- `start` and `stop` are local to the worker host. Do not run a worker's own `stop` or `restart` synchronously through its `rema run`; use `rema restart <name>` directly from the client.
- Keep the recorded working directory, config, and history. Do not use `rm` to upgrade. On restart failure, inspect the printed request directory's `log` and bootstrap job log. A stale `restart.lock` must only be removed after verifying that no helper or replacement startup is active.
